   "text": "I read this as zanamivir (the 4-guanidino-5-acetamido dihydropyran carboxylic acid with a glycerol side chain) and derived each stereocentre from the wedges and hashes.\n\n**Structure read from the drawing**\n- The ring is a six-membered dihydropyran: ring O, then C(COOH)=CH, then the CH bearing the guanidine, then the CH bearing the acetamide, then the CH attached to the ring O.\n- The carboxylic acid is on the ring carbon next to the ring O, and it is part of the C=C double bond.\n- The guanidine is attached through its imine nitrogen, as N=C(N)N.\n- The acetamide is NHC(=O)CH3.\n- The side chain is CH(OH)\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH2OH.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n\nI assigned each centre by viewing from the implicit hydrogen and converting to SMILES neighbour order.\n\n| Ring/side-chain centre | Drawn bond | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Carbon bearing the acetamide | hashed NH | `[C@@H]` |\n| Carbon bearing the guanidine | wedged N | `[C@@H]` |\n| Ring carbon next to the ring O | wedged ring O | `[C@H]` |\n| First side-chain carbon | wedged OH | `[C@H]` |\n| Second side-chain carbon | hashed OH | `[C@H]` |\n\nThe acetamide and guanidine are trans, which fits zanamivir. I derived the guanidine-bearing centre as `@@`, though the version of the zanamivir SMILES I remember has `@` there. I kept the derived assignment.\n\nSMILES: CC(=O)N[C@@H]1[C@@H](N=C(N)N)C=C(C(=O)O)O[C@H]1[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO"
